e  in the province of muller according to  authorities but many are warning that it  is still too early to give the all clear  because the conditions that have fueled  the fires in recent days haven't changed  it's still very hot very dry and very  windy now the wildfires here in turkey  are said to be the worst in decades they  have left so much destruction at least  eight people were killed and tens of  thousands had to leave their homes the  flames have turned huge tracts of  forests and fields into gray landscapes  according to the latest estimates a  total of about 150 000 hectares of land  has been destroyed nationwide an area  roughly two times the size of new york  city just to give you an  image an idea of the extent of the  damage  barbara the greek prime minister has  offered what sounds like an apology he's  promised compensation for those affected  by the fires is the government  acknowledging that it didn't prepare for  this properly  not openly and not really this sort of  halfway apology that we heard the day  before yesterday was just meant to sort  of calm down nerves to tell people i met  your sides but the government had put  out a forest protection plan this on in  june this year and of course nothing so  far nothing at all had been implemented  thousands of new policemen were employed  in greece but the country has only four  thousand firefighters overall for the  whole country and again here we saw  about the same amount of devastation as  in turkey was around 150 000 acres of  forests and fields having been burned so  yes they didn't take the threat  seriously even though it burns here  every summer they totally underestimated  the consequences of climate change and  now they sort of scrabbling to make good  but it's going to take a lot to to get  people back on their side and there is a  tremendous anger here in the villages  people are just furious when you even  mention the government  anger there in turkey well the uh uh in  in greece the